# Baseline security headers for every response on the real deploy.
#
# Lives here (not in netlify.toml) on purpose: Netlify applies
# netlify.toml's own [[headers]] blocks unconditionally, even under
# `netlify dev` -- but Vite's dev server injects an inline bootstrap
# script for Fast Refresh that a strict script-src blocks outright,
# which took the whole dev app down to a blank page. Vite copies
# everything in public/ verbatim into dist/ during a real build, so this
# file becomes dist/_headers and only applies to the actual deploy --
# never to `netlify dev`, which never touches dist/.
#
# CSP is written for this app's actual footprint (checked, not guessed):
# everything is same-origin, fonts are self-hosted, the only fetch target
# is this site's own function, and there's no dangerouslySetInnerHTML/eval
# anywhere -- so script-src can be a strict 'self' with no unsafe-inline.
# style-src keeps 'unsafe-inline' because React sets bar widths and grid
# column vars via the style prop; that's a CSS-only allowance (no script
# execution risk), not a loophole back to what script-src closes.
#
# img-src allows data: for one thing: the wave under the h1 is an inline
# SVG data URI in index.scss, not a file on disk. The scary reputation
# data: has is earned under script-src, where it would mean arbitrary
# code; under img-src it doesn't carry that. An SVG loaded *as an image*
# is inert -- browsers run no script in it and resolve no external refs,
# regardless of CSP -- and a data: URI makes no network request at all,
# so it can't exfiltrate. The contexts where an SVG would instead load as
# a live document stay shut: object-src is 'none', and frame-src is
# undeclared so it inherits default-src 'self'.
#
# Worth knowing before adding anything else this policy governs: NEITHER
# `netlify dev` NOR `vite preview` applies this file (the first never
# touches dist/, the second doesn't understand the _headers format), so
# production is the first place a new kind of source meets these rules.
# The squiggle above shipped green across every local check and rendered
# as a blank 20px gap on the live site for exactly that reason. To test a
# CSP change locally you have to serve dist/ yourself with the header set.
/*
  Content-Security-Policy: default-src 'self'; script-src 'self'; style-src 'self' 'unsafe-inline'; img-src 'self' data:; font-src 'self'; connect-src 'self'; base-uri 'self'; form-action 'self'; object-src 'none'; frame-ancestors 'none'
  Referrer-Policy: strict-origin-when-cross-origin
  X-Content-Type-Options: nosniff
  Permissions-Policy: camera=(), microphone=(), geolocation=()
